1935017</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="B6"><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><name><surname>Lynch</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Conery</surname><given-names>JS</given-names></name><article-title>The evolutionary fate and consequences of duplicate genes</article-title><source>Science</source><year>2000</year><volume>290</volume><issue>5494</issue><fpage>1151</fpage><lpage>1155</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1126/science.290.5494.1151</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11073452</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="B7"><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><name><surname>Koszul</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Caburet</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Dujon</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Fischer</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><article-title>Eucaryotic genome evolution through the spontaneous duplication of large chromosomal segments</article-title><source>Embo J</source><year>2004</year><volume>23</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>234</fpage><lpage>243</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/sj.emboj.7600024</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14685272</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="B8"><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><name><surname>Holland</surname><given-names>PW</given-names></name><article-title>Vertebrate evolution: something fishy about Hox genes</article-title><source>Curr Biol</source><year>1997</year><volume>7</volume><issue>9</issue><fpage>R570</fpage><lpage>572</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0960-9822(06)00284-3</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9285709</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="B9"><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><name><surname>Pebusque</surname><given-names>MJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Coulier</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Birnbaum</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Pontarotti</surname><given-